  "results": [
    {
      "section_id": "SG-CS-PRESCRIPTION-SPECIAL-RULES",
      "section_hash": "786aaa5c7a9b8583285edcf2a8fa8397cfd250fa373646abbc858ecd71cacecf",
      "disposition": "MINOR_EDIT",
      "severity": "Medium",
      "criteria": {
        "paraphrase_fidelity": "PASS",
        "material_exceptions": "PASS",
        "ma_vs_federal_distinctions": "PASS",
        "actor_and_scope": "PASS",
        "deadlines": "FAIL",
        "forms": "PASS",
        "decision_logic": "FAIL",
        "overgeneralization": "PASS",
        "unsupported_propositions": "PASS",
        "practice_question_mapping": "PASS",
        "study_usefulness_without_legal_distortion": "PASS"
      },
      "findings": [
        {
          "severity": "Medium",
          "criterion": "deadlines",
          "location": "orientation[5].text; comparison_tables[1].rows[3].cells[1]; comparison_tables[6].rows[2].cells[3]; timing_deadlines[4].consequence; quick_review[9].text; retrieval_prompts[11].answer; common_traps[8].text",
          "problem": "For a Schedule II prescription from a nonresident (out-of-state) prescriber that carries a do-not-fill-before date, the section gives the Massachusetts window only as 'may be filled only if issued within the preceding five days' (or 'runs instead on a five-day filling window'), and when it relies on the Board of Registration in Pharmacy's December 2025 newsletter it reports only the newsletter's Massachusetts-prescriber sentence. The same newsletter item goes on to state that Schedule II prescriptions issued by a nonresident prescriber are only valid for five days from the date of issuance or the do-not-fill-before date, as applicable. The section never says that the five-day period for a staged prescription runs from its do-not-fill-before date, and it confines the do-not-fill-before reading to 'the date of issuance referred to in section 23'. Read with its own teaching that every prescription in a set is dated as of, and issued on, the day of the visit under 21 CFR 1306.05(a), a candidate will count the five days from the visit and conclude that a later prescription in a nonresident prescriber's set lapses before its earliest-fill date, so it can never be filled in Massachusetts. common_traps[8] states the five-day rule in exactly that context.",
          "rationale": "M.G.L. c. 94C, s. 18(d) and (d1/2) authorize filling only prescriptions 'issued within the preceding five days' and do not say how a do-not-fill-before date affects that. Current Massachusetts guidance answers it the same way for both windows: the December 2025 Board newsletter measures the Massachusetts 30 days and the nonresident five days from the date of issuance or the do-not-fill-before date, as applicable, and the Board of Registration in Medicine guideline treats the do-not-fill-before date as the date of issuance. The section declares the multiple-prescription validity question in scope (subtopic; learning objective 4) and chose to state the out-of-state window in its own tables, timing entries, prompts and traps, so the clock it gives there has to start from the right event. As written, it leads to the wrong result on a Massachusetts fact pattern the Board has highlighted. A minor edit fixes it: say, framed as guidance in the way the section already frames the 30-day reading, that the Board measures a nonresident prescriber's five days from the do-not-fill-before date where there is one, and make the listed fields consistent. The rest of the section's 30-day validity teaching, and its account of the authority and effect of both guidance documents for Massachusetts prescribers, is accurate.",
