      "summary": "MINOR_EDIT, Medium. I checked every field of the section against current official text; all but one point is correct. Verified accurate: the 21 CFR 1306.11(a) fax default (an ordinary fax is an advance copy, and the original manually signed prescription must be reviewed before the actual dispensing) and the three exceptions in (e), (f) and (g). Each exception is matched to its drug and patient conditions, with (e) naming no setting, (f) needing no narcotic, and the hospice notation in (g). The five conditions of 1306.12(b)(1) and (b)(2); dating under 1306.05(a), which the section quotes accurately from the current text; and the pharmacist's bar in 1306.14(e). The Massachusetts 30-day validity under s. 23(a) as the 2008 Board of Registration in Medicine guideline and the December 2025 Board of Registration in Pharmacy newsletter apply it, with their authority described accurately as guidance. 1306.23 partial filling, which is kept apart from Schedule II partial fills under 1306.13(b) and s. 18(d3/4) (a pharmacist 'shall' dispense a lesser quantity at the patient's request), and apart from a pharmacist splitting a prescription. s. 23(b)-(e) and Policy 2024-05 (adopted 9/5/24): endorsement on the face, the separate file for written prescriptions, the categories and 90/30-day single-filling ceilings, the Department's power to lower them, and two-year pharmacy retention. The current 1306.07(a)-(d) and (f): (e) is now reserved, and the new (g)-(h) on emergency medical services add no prescribing route. The 2023 three-day rule. The Massachusetts layer of s. 9(b) and 105 CMR 700.010(A) with the 700.001 definition of Sample Medication. Each drug example's schedule, checked one substance at a time. All 21 practice questions (MA-Q-0099, 0119, 0154-0159, 0165, 0166, 0185-0188, 0192-0197, 0383) can be answered from the section, and nothing in it contradicts any key or explanation. The one defect, recorded as two findings: for a nonresident prescriber's Schedule II prescription with a do-not-fill-before date, the section states the Massachusetts window only as 'issued within the preceding five days', and decision_logic[8] and role_duties[5] apply that to set prescriptions presented on or after their indicated date. Read with the section's dating teaching, that bars every later prescription in such a set. The December 2025 Board newsletter the section itself relies on measures the nonresident five days from the date of issuance or the do-not-fill-before date, as applicable. Not recorded as defects: the example of 'a limit on an initial opioid prescription where one applies' is hedged (s. 19D(c) excludes medications for opioid dependence); the 30-day window of s. 18(c) for out-of-state Schedule III-VI prescriptions lies outside the declared scope; and reading 1306.07(b) so that any further supply renews or extends the emergency treatment is consistent with the current text."
